  "description": "Update k-means centroids from a mini-batch using the specified assignment and incremental-mean rule.",
  "theory": "## What Is Mini-Batch k-Means?\n\nIn classical machine learning, algorithms extract predictive decision boundaries, cluster structures, or low-dimensional manifolds from structured feature tables.\n\nUpdate k-means centroids from a mini-batch using the specified assignment and incremental-mean rule.\n\n---\n\n## Mathematical Formulation & Derivation\n\nBalancing model capacity against regularization prevents overfitting on finite training sets and improves out-of-sample generalization.\n\n---\n\n## Key Properties & Conceptual Intuition\n\n- Implement the mathematical or algorithmic behavior described above.\n- Match the function signature and output structure in starterCode.\n- **Related Topics in This Track:** **Leaky ReLU**, **ReLU**, **Sigmoid**.\n\n---\n\n## Numerical Stability & Edge Cases\n\n- **Boundary & Shape Consistency:** Ensure 1D vectors, 2D matrices, and single-element inputs preserve their expected output dimensions and rounding precision.\n\n---\n\n## Computational Complexity\n\n- **Time Complexity:** $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ where $N$ is the total number of input elements, executing in a single vectorized pass.\n- **Space Complexity:** $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ to hold the output array (or $\\mathcal{O}(1)$ auxiliary working memory beyond the result).",
